Unix utility 'syslogd'

The Unix utility 'syslogd' sends a message to the system log, from a utility or from a
script, so that components outside the kernel can record what they did. The message is given
as the parameter and goes to the log with the identification of the process that sent it.

Messages sent this way are kept apart from the ones the kernel itself records, which are the
ones the 'dmesg' utility lists.

USAGE:

syslogd <message>

Example of use:

syslogd Volume check finished

It records that line in the system log.

For more information, enter 'syslogd ?' or 'syslogd --help'.

HISTORY

This command appeared in the Hexagonix H2 or in previous development versions (2022).

